20.1  Which package owns a file

TL;DR
  - Every file a package installs is recorded in the package database: ask it
    who owns a file.
  - dpkg -S on Debian and Ubuntu, rpm -qf on Fedora and openSUSE, pacman -Qo on
    Arch.
  - For a program that isn't installed yet, search the repositories instead (see
    19.2).

      dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl
      rpm -qf /usr/bin/curl
      pacman -Qo /usr/bin/curl

On your machine
  dpkg      2 packages
  Snap      2 snaps
  Flatpak   3 apps

Pitfalls
  - Give the full path: dpkg -S curl matches every path that contains "curl".
    command -v curl gives the path.
  - Files you created, and settings a package generated after install, belong to
    no package.
  - With merged /usr (see 9.1), ask about /usr/bin/… rather than /bin/…: the
    database may only know one of the two.

Deep dive

  ▾ What's in the package database?

    For each package: its version, its dependencies, and the list of its files
    with checksums. That's how dpkg --verify, rpm -V and pacman -Qkk can tell
    which installed files were changed since installation.

  ▾ Why does it matter?

    Before editing or deleting a file under /usr or /etc, knowing its package
    tells you whether an update will overwrite your change, or bring a deleted
    file back. Changes belong in your own files: a drop-in, a file in /etc, or
    /usr/local.

Related
  Commands  dpkg, rpm, pacman
  See also  idk 20.2 Native packages vs Snap and Flatpak
  See also  idk 19.2 ELF and "command not found"
